include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://                   stb_alloc - hierarchical allocator
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://                                     inspired by http://swapped.cc/halloc
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// When you alloc a given block through stb_alloc, you have these choices:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://       1. does it have a parent?
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://       2. can it have children?
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://       3. can it be freed directly?
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://       4. is it transferrable?
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://       5. what is its alignment?
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Here are interesting combinations of those:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://                              children   free    transfer     alignment
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  arena                          Y         Y         N           n/a
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  no-overhead, chunked           N         N         N         normal
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  string pool alloc              N         N         N            1
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  parent-ptr, chunked            Y         N         N         normal
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  low-overhead, unchunked        N         Y         Y         normal
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://  general purpose alloc          Y         Y         Y         normal
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Unchunked allocations will probably return 16-aligned pointers. If
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// we 16-align the results, we have room for 4 pointers. For smaller
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// allocations that allow finer alignment, we can reduce the pointers.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// The strategy is that given a pointer, assuming it has a header (only
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// the no-overhead allocations have no header), we can determine the
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// type of the header fields, and the number of them, by stepping backwards
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// through memory and looking at the tags in the bottom bits.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Implementation strategy:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     chunked allocations come from the middle of chunks, and can't
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     be freed. thefore they do not need to be on a sibling chain.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     they may need child pointers if they have children.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// chunked, with-children
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *parent;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// unchunked, no-children -- reduced storage
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *next_sibling;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *prev_sibling_nextp;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// unchunked, general
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *first_child;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *next_sibling;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *prev_sibling_nextp;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://     void *chunks;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// so, if we code each of these fields with different bit patterns
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// (actually same one for next/prev/child), then we can identify which
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// each one is from the last field.
