include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://                  stb_pointer_set
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// For data structures that support querying by key, data structure
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// classes always hand-wave away the issue of what to do if two entries
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// have the same key: basically, store a linked list of all the nodes
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// which have the same key (a LISP-style list).
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// The thing is, it's not that trivial. If you have an O(log n)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// lookup data structure, but then n/4 items have the same value,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// you don't want to spend O(n) time scanning that list when
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// deleting an item if you already have a pointer to the item.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// (You have to spend O(n) time enumerating all the items with
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// a given key, sure, and you can't accelerate deleting a particular
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// item if you only have the key, not a pointer to the item.)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// I'm going to call this data structure, whatever it turns out to
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// be, a "pointer set", because we don't store any associated data for
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// items in this data structure, we just answer the question of
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// whether an item is in it or not (it's effectively one bit per pointer).
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Technically they don't have to be pointers; you could cast ints
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// to (void *) if you want, but you can't store 0 or 1 because of the
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// hash table.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Since the fastest data structure we might want to add support for
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// identical-keys to is a hash table with O(1)-ish lookup time,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// that means that the conceptual "linked list of all items with
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// the same indexed value" that we build needs to have the same
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// performance; that way when we index a table we think is arbitrary
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// ints, but in fact half of them are 0, we don't get screwed.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Therefore, it needs to be a hash table, at least when it gets
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// large. On the other hand, when the data has totally arbitrary ints
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// or floats, there won't be many collisions, and we'll have tons of
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// 1-item bitmaps. That will be grossly inefficient as hash tables;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// trade-off; the hash table is reasonably efficient per-item when
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// it's large, but not when it's small. So we need to do something
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Judy-like and use different strategies depending on the size.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h:// Like Judy, we'll use the bottom bit to encode the strategy:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://      bottom bits:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://          00     -   direct pointer
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://          01     -   4-item bucket (16 bytes, no length, NULLs)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://          10     -   N-item array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stb.h://          11     -   hash table
