include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// stretchy_buffer.h - v1.04 - public domain - nothings.org/stb
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// a vector<>-like dynamic array for C
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// version history:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      1.04 -  fix warning
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      1.03 -  compile as C++ maybe
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      1.02 -  tweaks to syntax for no good reason
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      1.01 -  added a "common uses" documentation section
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      1.0  -  fixed bug in the version I posted prematurely
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://      0.9  -  rewrite to try to avoid strict-aliasing optimization
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://              issues, but won't compile as C++
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// Will probably not work correctly with strict-aliasing optimizations.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// The idea:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    This implements an approximation to C++ vector<> for C, in that it
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    provides a generic definition for dynamic arrays which you can
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    still access in a typesafe way using arr[i] or *(arr+i). However,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    it is simply a convenience wrapper around the common idiom of
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    of keeping a set of variables (in a struct or globals) which store
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://        - pointer to array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://        - the length of the "in-use" part of the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://        - the current size of the allocated array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    I find it to be the single most useful non-built-in-structure when
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    programming in C (hash tables a close second), but to be clear
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    it lacks many of the capabilities of C++ vector<>: there is no
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    range checking, the object address isn't stable (see next section
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    for details), the set of methods available is small (although
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    the file stb.h has another implementation of stretchy buffers
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    called 'stb_arr' which provides more methods, e.g. for insertion
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    and deletion).
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// How to use:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    Unlike other stb header file libraries, there is no need to
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    define an _IMPLEMENTATION symbol. Every #include creates as
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    much implementation is needed.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    stretchy_buffer.h does not define any types, so you do not
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    need to #include it to before defining data types that are
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    stretchy buffers, only in files that *manipulate* stretchy
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    buffers.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    If you want a stretchy buffer aka dynamic array containing
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    objects of TYPE, declare such an array as:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       TYPE *myarray = NULL;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    (There is no typesafe way to distinguish between stretchy
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    buffers and regular arrays/pointers; this is necessary to
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    make ordinary array indexing work on these objects.)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    Unlike C++ vector<>, the stretchy_buffer has the same
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    semantics as an object that you manually malloc and realloc.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    The pointer may relocate every time you add a new object
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    to it, so you:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         1. can't take long-term pointers to elements of the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         2. have to return the pointer from functions which might expand it
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://            (either as a return value or by storing it to a ptr-to-ptr)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    Now you can do the following things with this array:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         sb_free(TYPE *a)           free the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         sb_count(TYPE *a)          the number of elements in the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         sb_push(TYPE *a, TYPE v)   adds v on the end of the array, a la push_back
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         sb_add(TYPE *a, int n)     adds n uninitialized elements at end of array & returns pointer to first added
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         sb_last(TYPE *a)           returns an lvalue of the last item in the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://         a[n]                       access the nth (counting from 0) element of the array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     #define STRETCHY_BUFFER_NO_SHORT_NAMES to only export
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     names of the form 'stb_sb_' if you have a name that would
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     otherwise collide.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     Note that these are all macros and many of them evaluate
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     their arguments more than once, so the arguments should
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     be side-effect-free.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     Note that 'TYPE *a' in sb_push and sb_add must be lvalues
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     so that the library can overwrite the existing pointer if
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     the object has to be reallocated.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     In an out-of-memory condition, the code will try to
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     set up a null-pointer or otherwise-invalid-pointer
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     exception to happen later. It's possible optimizing
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     compilers could detect this write-to-null statically
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     and optimize away some of the code, but it should only
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     be along the failure path. Nevertheless, for more security
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     in the face of such compilers, #define STRETCHY_BUFFER_OUT_OF_MEMORY
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     to a statement such as assert(0) or exit(1) or something
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://     to force a failure when out-of-memory occurs.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// Common use:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    The main application for this is when building a list of
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    things with an unknown quantity, either due to loading from
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    a file or through a process which produces an unpredictable
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    number.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    My most common idiom is something like:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       SomeStruct *arr = NULL;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       while (something)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          SomeStruct new_one;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one.whatever = whatever;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one.whatup   = whatup;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one.foobar   = barfoo;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          sb_push(arr, new_one);
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       }
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    and various closely-related factorings of that. For example,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    you might have several functions to create/init new SomeStructs,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    and if you use the above idiom, you might prefer to make them
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    return structs rather than take non-const-pointers-to-structs,
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    so you can do things like:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       SomeStruct *arr = NULL;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       while (something)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          if (case_A) {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://             sb_push(arr, some_func1());
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          } else if (case_B) {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://             sb_push(arr, some_func2());
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          } else {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://             sb_push(arr, some_func3());
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          }
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       }
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    Note that the above relies on the fact that sb_push doesn't
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    evaluate its second argument more than once. The macros do
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    evaluate the *array* argument multiple times, and numeric
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    arguments may be evaluated multiple times, but you can rely
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    on the second argument of sb_push being evaluated only once.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    Of course, you don't have to store bare objects in the array;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    if you need the objects to have stable pointers, store an array
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    of pointers instead:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       SomeStruct **arr = NULL;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       while (something)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       {
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          SomeStruct *new_one = malloc(sizeof(*new_one));
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one->whatever = whatever;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one->whatup   = whatup;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          new_one->foobar   = barfoo;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://          sb_push(arr, new_one);
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://       }
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// How it works:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    A long-standing tradition in things like malloc implementations
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    is to store extra data before the beginning of the block returned
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    to the user. The stretchy buffer implementation here uses the
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    same trick; the current-count and current-allocation-size are
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    stored before the beginning of the array returned to the user.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    (This means you can't directly free() the pointer, because the
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    allocated pointer is different from the type-safe pointer provided
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    to the user.)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    The details are trivial and implementation is straightforward;
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    the main trick is in realizing in the first place that it's
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://    possible to do this in a generic, type-safe way in C.
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// Contributors:
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// Timothy Wright (github:ZenToad)
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h:// LICENSE
include/stb-master/deprecated/stretchy_buffer.h://
