include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h:// FAQ
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What counts as a tilemap for this library?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: An array of rectangles, where each rectangle contains a small
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      stack of images.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What are the limitations?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Maps are limited to 4096x4096 in dimension.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      Each map square can only contain a stack of at most 32 images.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      A map can only use up to 32768 distinct image tiles.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How do I compile this?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: You need to #define several symbols before #including it, but only
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      in one file. This will cause all the function definitions to be
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      generated in that file. See the "HOW TO COMPILE" section.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What advantages does this have over a standalone editor?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: For one, you can integrate the editor into your game so you can
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      flip between editing and testing without even switching windows.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      For another, you don't need an XML parser to get at the map data.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: Can I live-edit my game maps?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Not really, the editor keeps its own map representation.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How do I save and load maps?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: You have to do this yourself. The editor provides serialization
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      functions (get & set) for reading and writing the map it holds.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      You can choose whatever format you want to store the map to on
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      disk; you just need to provide functions to convert. (For example,
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      I actually store the editor's map representation to disk basically
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      as-is; then I have a single function that converts from the editor
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      map representation to the game representation, which is used both
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to go from editor-to-game and from loaded-map-to-game.)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: I want to have tiles change appearance based on what's
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      adjacent, or other tile-display/substitution trickiness.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: You can do this when you convert from the editor's map
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      representation to the game representation, but there's
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      no way to show this live in the editor.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: The editor appears to be put map location (0,0) at the top left?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      I want to use a different coordinate system in my game (e.g. y
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      increasing upwards, or origin at the center).
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: You can do this when you convert from the editor's map
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      representation to the game representation. (Don't forget to
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      translate link coordinates as well!)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: The editor appears to put pixel (0,0) at the top left? I want
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to use a different coordinate system in my game.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: The editor defines an "editor pixel coordinate system" with
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      (0,0) at the top left and requires you to display things in
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      that coordinate system. You can freely remap those coordinates
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to anything you want on screen.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How do I scale the user interface?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Since you do all the rendering, you can scale up all the rendering
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      calls that the library makes to you. If you do, (a) you need
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to also scale up the mouse coordinates, and (b) you may want
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to scale the map display back down so that you're only scaling
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      the UI and not everything. See the next question.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How do I scale the map display?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Use stbte_set_spacing() to change the size that the map is displayed
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      at. Note that the "callbacks" to draw tiles are used for both drawing
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      the map and drawing the tile palette, so that callback may need to
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      draw at two different scales. You should choose the scales to match
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://       You can tell them apart because the
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      tile palette gets NULL for the property pointer.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How does object editing work?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: One way to think of this is that in the editor, you're placing
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      spawners, not objects. Each spawner must be tile-aligned, because
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      it's only a tile editor. Each tile (stack of layers) gets
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      an associated set of properties, and it's up to you to
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      determine what properties should appear for a given tile,
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      based on e.g. the spawners that are in it.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How are properties themselves handled?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: All properties, regardless of UI behavior, are internally floats.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      Each tile has an array of floats associated with it, which is
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      passed back to you when drawing the tiles so you can draw
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      objects appropriately modified by the properties.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What if I want to have two different objects/spawners in
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      one tile, both of which have their own properties?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Make sure STBTE_MAX_PROPERTIES is large enough for the sum of
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      properties in both objects, and then you have to explicitly
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      map the property slot #s to the appropriate objects. They'll
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      still all appear in a single property panel; there's no way
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      to get multiple panels.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: Can I do one-to-many linking?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: The library only supports one link per tile. However, you
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      can have multiple tiles all link to a single tile. So, you
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      can fake one-to-many linking by linking in the reverse
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      direction.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What if I have two objects in the same tile, and they each
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      need an independent link? Or I have two kinds of link associated
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      with a single object?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: There is no way to do this. (Unless you can reverse one link.)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: How does cut & paste interact with object properties & links?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Currently the library has no idea which properties or links
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      are associated with which layers of a tile. So currently, the
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      library will only copy properties & links if the layer panel
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      is set to allow all layers to be copied, OR if you set the
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      "props" in the layer panel to "always". Similarly, you can
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      set "props" to "none" so it will never copy.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What happens if the library gets a memory allocation failure
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      while I'm editing? Will I lose my work?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: The library allocates all editor memory when you create
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      the tilemap. It allocates a maximally-sized map and a
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      fixed-size undo buffer (and the fixed-size copy buffer
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      is static), and never allocates memory while it's running.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      So it can't fail due to running out of memory.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Q: What happens if the library crashes while I'm editing? Will
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      I lose my work?
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   A: Yes. Save often.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h:// HOW TO COMPILE
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   This header file contains both the header file and the
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   implementation file in one. To create the implementation,
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   in one source file define a few symbols first and then
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   include this header:
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STB_TILEMAP_EDITOR_IMPLEMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this triggers the implementation
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      void STBTE_DRAW_RECT(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, unsigned int color);
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this must draw a filled rectangle (exclusive on right/bottom)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // color = (r<<16)|(g<<8)|(b)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      void STBTE_DRAW_TILE(int x0, int y0,
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://                    unsigned short id, int highlight, float *data);
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this draws the tile image identified by 'id' in one of several
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // highlight modes (see STBTE_drawmode_* in the header section);
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // if 'data' is NULL, it's drawing the tile in the palette; if 'data'
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // is not NULL, it's drawing a tile on the map, and that is the data
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // associated with that map tile
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #include "stb_tilemap_editor.h"
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Optionally you can define the following functions before the include;
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   note these must be macros (but they can just call a function) so
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   this library can #ifdef to detect if you've defined them:
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_PROP_TYPE(int n, short *tiledata, float *params) ...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // Returns the type of the n'th property of a given tile, which
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // controls how it is edited. Legal types are:
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //     0                    /* no editable property in this slot */
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //     STBTE_PROP_int       /* uses a slider to adjust value     */
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //     STBTE_PROP_float     /* uses a weird multi-axis control   */
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //     STBTE_PROP_bool      /* uses a checkbox to change value   */
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // And you can bitwise-OR in the following flags:
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //     STBTE_PROP_disabled
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // Note that all of these are stored as floats in the param array.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // The integer slider is limited in precision based on the space
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // available on screen, so for wide-ranged integers you may want
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // to use floats instead.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // Since the tiledata is passed to you, you can choose which property
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // is bound to that slot based on that data.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      //
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // Changing the type of a parameter does not cause the underlying
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // value to be clamped to the type min/max except when the tile is
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // explicitly selected.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_PROP_NAME(int n, short *tiledata, float *params) ...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // these return a string with the name for slot #n in the float
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // property list for the tile.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_PROP_MIN(int n, short *tiledata) ...your code here...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_PROP_MAX(int n, short *tiledata) ...your code here...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // These return the allowable range for the property values for
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // the specified slot. It is never called for boolean types.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_PROP_FLOAT_SCALE(int n, short *tiledata, float *params)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // This rescales the float control for a given property; by default
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // left mouse drags add integers, right mouse drags adds fractions,
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // but you can rescale this per-property.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_FLOAT_CONTROL_GRANULARITY       ... value ...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // This returns the number of pixels of mouse motion necessary
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // to advance the object float control. Default is 4
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_ALLOW_LINK(short *src, float *src_data,  \
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://                               short *dest, float *dest_data) ...your code...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this returns true or false depending on whether you allow a link
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // to be drawn from a tile 'src' to a tile 'dest'. if you don't
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // define this, linking will not be supported
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_LINK_COLOR(short *src, float *src_data,  \
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://                               short *dest, float *dest_data) ...your code...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // return a color encoded as a 24-bit unsigned integer in the
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // form 0xRRGGBB. If you don't define this, default colors will
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // be used.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      [[ support for those below is not implemented yet ]]
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_HITTEST_TILE(x0,y0,id,mx,my)   ...your code here...
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this returns true or false depending on whether the mouse
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // pointer at mx,my is over (touching) a tile of type 'id'
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // displayed at x0,y0. Normally stb_tilemap_editor just does
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // this hittest based on the tile geometry, but if you have
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      // tiles whose images extend out of the tile, you'll need this.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h:// ADDITIONAL CONFIGURATION
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   The following symbols set static limits which determine how much
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   memory will be allocated for the editor. You can override them
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   by making similar definitions, but memory usage will increase.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_TILEMAP_X      200   // max 4096
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_TILEMAP_Y      200   // max 4096
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_LAYERS         8     // max 32
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_CATEGORIES     100
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_UNDO_BUFFER_BYTES  (1 << 24) // 16 MB
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_COPY           90000  // e.g. 300x300
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://      #define STBTE_MAX_PROPERTIES     10     // max properties per tile
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h:// API
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   Further documentation appears in the header-file section below.
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h:// EDITING MULTIPLE LEVELS
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   You can only have one active editor instance. To switch between multiple
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   levels, you can either store the levels in your own format and copy them
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   in and out of the editor format, or you can create multiple stbte_tilemap
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   objects and switch between them. The latter has the advantage that each
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   stbte_tilemap keeps its own undo state. (The clipboard is global, so
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://   either approach allows cut&pasting between levels.)
include/stb-master/stb_tilemap_editor.h://
