include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:/* stb_image_resize - v0.96 - public domain image resizing
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   by Jorge L Rodriguez (@VinoBS) - 2014
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   http://github.com/nothings/stb
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   Written with emphasis on usability, portability, and efficiency. (No
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   SIMD or threads, so it be easily outperformed by libs that use those.)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   Only scaling and translation is supported, no rotations or shears.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   Easy API downsamples w/Mitchell filter, upsamples w/cubic interpolation.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   COMPILING & LINKING
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      In one C/C++ file that #includes this file, do this:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         #define STB_IMAGE_RESIZE_IMPLEMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      before the #include. That will create the implementation in that file.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   QUICKSTART
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      stbir_resize_uint8(      input_pixels , in_w , in_h , 0,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               output_pixels, out_w, out_h, 0, num_channels)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      stbir_resize_float(...)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      stbir_resize_uint8_srgb( input_pixels , in_w , in_h , 0,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               output_pixels, out_w, out_h, 0,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               num_channels , alpha_chan  , 0)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      stbir_resize_uint8_srgb_edgemode(
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               input_pixels , in_w , in_h , 0, 
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               output_pixels, out_w, out_h, 0, 
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                               num_channels , alpha_chan  , 0, STBIR_EDGE_CLAMP)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:                                                            // WRAP/REFLECT/ZERO
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   FULL API
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      See the "header file" section of the source for API documentation.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:   ADDITIONAL DOCUMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      SRGB & FLOATING POINT REPRESENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         The sRGB functions presume IEEE floating point. If you do not have
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         IEEE floating point, define STBIR_NON_IEEE_FLOAT. This will use
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         a slower implementation.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      MEMORY ALLOCATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         The resize functions here perform a single memory allocation using
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         malloc. To control the memory allocation, before the #include that
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         triggers the implementation, do:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_MALLOC(size,context) ...
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_FREE(ptr,context)   ...
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         Each resize function makes exactly one call to malloc/free, so to use
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         temp memory, store the temp memory in the context and return that.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      ASSERT
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         Define STBIR_ASSERT(boolval) to override assert() and not use assert.h
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      OPTIMIZATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         Define STBIR_SATURATE_INT to compute clamp values in-range using
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         integer operations instead of float operations. This may be faster
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         on some platforms.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      DEFAULT FILTERS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         For functions which don't provide explicit control over what filters
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         to use, you can change the compile-time defaults with
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_DEFAULT_FILTER_UPSAMPLE     STBIR_FILTER_something
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_DEFAULT_FILTER_DOWNSAMPLE   STBIR_FILTER_something
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         See stbir_filter in the header-file section for the list of filters.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      NEW FILTERS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         A number of 1D filter kernels are used. For a list of
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         supported filters see the stbir_filter enum. To add a new filter,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         write a filter function and add it to stbir__filter_info_table.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      PROGRESS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         For interactive use with slow resize operations, you can install
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         a progress-report callback:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_PROGRESS_REPORT(val)   some_func(val)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         The parameter val is a float which goes from 0 to 1 as progress is made.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         For example:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            static void my_progress_report(float progress);
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STBIR_PROGRESS_REPORT(val) my_progress_report(val)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #define STB_IMAGE_RESIZE_IMPLEMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            #include "stb_image_resize.h"
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            static void my_progress_report(float progress)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            {
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:               printf("Progress: %f%%\n", progress*100);
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:            }
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      MAX CHANNELS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         If your image has more than 64 channels, define STBIR_MAX_CHANNELS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         to the max you'll have.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:      ALPHA CHANNEL
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         Most of the resizing functions provide the ability to control how
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         the alpha channel of an image is processed. The important things
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         to know about this:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         1. The best mathematically-behaved version of alpha to use is
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         called "premultiplied alpha", in which the other color channels
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         have had the alpha value multiplied in. If you use premultiplied
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         alpha, linear filtering (such as image resampling done by this
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         library, or performed in texture units on GPUs) does the "right
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         thing". While premultiplied alpha is standard in the movie CGI
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         industry, it is still uncommon in the videogame/real-time world.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         If you linearly filter non-premultiplied alpha, strange effects
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         occur. (For example, the 50/50 average of 99% transparent bright green
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         and 1% transparent black produces 50% transparent dark green when
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         non-premultiplied, whereas premultiplied it produces 50%
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         transparent near-black. The former introduces green energy
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         that doesn't exist in the source image.)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         2. Artists should not edit premultiplied-alpha images; artists
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         want non-premultiplied alpha images. Thus, art tools generally output
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         non-premultiplied alpha images.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         3. You will get best results in most cases by converting images
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         to premultiplied alpha before processing them mathematically.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         4. If you pass the flag STBIR_FLAG_ALPHA_PREMULTIPLIED, the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         resizer does not do anything special for the alpha channel;
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         it is resampled identically to other channels. This produces
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         the correct results for premultiplied-alpha images, but produces
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         less-than-ideal results for non-premultiplied-alpha images.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         5. If you do not pass the flag STBIR_FLAG_ALPHA_PREMULTIPLIED,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         then the resizer weights the contribution of input pixels
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         based on their alpha values, or, equivalently, it multiplies
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         the alpha value into the color channels, resamples, then divides
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         by the resultant alpha value. Input pixels which have alpha=0 do
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         not contribute at all to output pixels unless _all_ of the input
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         pixels affecting that output pixel have alpha=0, in which case
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         the result for that pixel is the same as it would be without
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         STBIR_FLAG_ALPHA_PREMULTIPLIED. However, this is only true for
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         input images in integer formats. For input images in float format,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         input pixels with alpha=0 have no effect, and output pixels
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         which have alpha=0 will be 0 in all channels. (For float images,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         you can manually achieve the same result by adding a tiny epsilon
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         value to the alpha channel of every image, and then subtracting
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize_test/old_image_resize.h:         or clamping it at the end.)
