include/stb-master/stb_image.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// stb_image uses ints pervasively, including for offset calculations.
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// therefore the largest decoded image size we can support with the
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// current code, even on 64-bit targets, is INT_MAX. this is not a
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// significant limitation for the intended use case.
include/stb-master/stb_image.h://
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// we do, however, need to make sure our size calculations don't
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// overflow. hence a few helper functions for size calculations that
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// multiply integers together, making sure that they're non-negative
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// and no overflow occurs.
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// return 1 if the sum is valid, 0 on overflow.
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:// negative terms are considered invalid.
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:static int stbi__addsizes_valid(int a, int b)
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:{
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:   if (b < 0) return 0;
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:   // now 0 <= b <= INT_MAX, hence also
include/stb-master/stb_image.h:   // 0 <= INT_MAX - b <= INTMAX.
