include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// USAGE
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Include this file in whatever places need to refer to it. In ONE C/C++
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   file, write:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      #define STB_TRUETYPE_IMPLEMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   before the #include of this file. This expands out the actual
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   implementation into that C/C++ file.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   To make the implementation private to the file that generates the implementation,
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      #define STBTT_STATIC
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Simple 3D API (don't ship this, but it's fine for tools and quick start)
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_BakeFontBitmap()               -- bake a font to a bitmap for use as texture
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetBakedQuad()                 -- compute quad to draw for a given char
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Improved 3D API (more shippable):
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           #include "stb_rect_pack.h"           -- optional, but you really want it
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_PackBegin()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_PackSetOversampling()          -- for improved quality on small fonts
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_PackFontRanges()               -- pack and renders
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_PackEnd()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetPackedQuad()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   "Load" a font file from a memory buffer (you have to keep the buffer loaded)
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_InitFont()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetFontOffsetForIndex()        -- indexing for TTC font collections
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetNumberOfFonts()             -- number of fonts for TTC font collections
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Render a unicode codepoint to a bitmap
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetCodepointBitmap()           -- allocates and returns a bitmap
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_MakeCodepointBitmap()          -- renders into bitmap you provide
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetCodepointBitmapBox()        -- how big the bitmap must be
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Character advance/positioning
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetCodepointHMetrics()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetFontVMetrics()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetFontVMetricsOS2()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://           stbtt_GetCodepointKernAdvance()
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Starting with version 1.06, the rasterizer was replaced with a new,
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   faster and generally-more-precise rasterizer. The new rasterizer more
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   accurately measures pixel coverage for anti-aliasing, except in the case
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   where multiple shapes overlap, in which case it overestimates the AA pixel
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   coverage. Thus, anti-aliasing of intersecting shapes may look wrong. If
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   this turns out to be a problem, you can re-enable the old rasterizer with
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://        #define STBTT_RASTERIZER_VERSION 1
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   which will incur about a 15% speed hit.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// ADDITIONAL DOCUMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Immediately after this block comment are a series of sample programs.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   After the sample programs is the "header file" section. This section
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   includes documentation for each API function.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Some important concepts to understand to use this library:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Codepoint
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         Characters are defined by unicode codepoints, e.g. 65 is
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         uppercase A, 231 is lowercase c with a cedilla, 0x7e30 is
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         the hiragana for "ma".
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Glyph
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         A visual character shape (every codepoint is rendered as
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         some glyph)
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Glyph index
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         A font-specific integer ID representing a glyph
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Baseline
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         Glyph shapes are defined relative to a baseline, which is the
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         bottom of uppercase characters. Characters extend both above
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         and below the baseline.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Current Point
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         As you draw text to the screen, you keep track of a "current point"
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         which is the origin of each character. The current point's vertical
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         position is the baseline. Even "baked fonts" use this model.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Vertical Font Metrics
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         The vertical qualities of the font, used to vertically position
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         and space the characters. See docs for stbtt_GetFontVMetrics.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      Font Size in Pixels or Points
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         The preferred interface for specifying font sizes in stb_truetype
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         is to specify how tall the font's vertical extent should be in pixels.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         If that sounds good enough, skip the next paragraph.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         Most font APIs instead use "points", which are a common typographic
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         measurement for describing font size, defined as 72 points per inch.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         stb_truetype provides a point API for compatibility. However, true
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         "per inch" conventions don't make much sense on computer displays
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         since different monitors have different number of pixels per
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         inch. For example, Windows traditionally uses a convention that
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         there are 96 pixels per inch, thus making 'inch' measurements have
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         nothing to do with inches, and thus effectively defining a point to
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         be 1.333 pixels. Additionally, the TrueType font data provides
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         an explicit scale factor to scale a given font's glyphs to points,
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         but the author has observed that this scale factor is often wrong
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         for non-commercial fonts, thus making fonts scaled in points
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://         according to the TrueType spec incoherently sized in practice.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// DETAILED USAGE:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://  Scale:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    Select how high you want the font to be, in points or pixels.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    Call ScaleForPixelHeight or ScaleForMappingEmToPixels to compute
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    a scale factor SF that will be used by all other functions.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://  Baseline:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    You need to select a y-coordinate that is the baseline of where
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    your text will appear. Call GetFontBoundingBox to get the baseline-relative
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    bounding box for all characters. SF*-y0 will be the distance in pixels
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    that the worst-case character could extend above the baseline, so if
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    you want the top edge of characters to appear at the top of the
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    screen where y=0, then you would set the baseline to SF*-y0.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://  Current point:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    Set the current point where the first character will appear. The
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    first character could extend left of the current point; this is font
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    dependent. You can either choose a current point that is the leftmost
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    point and hope, or add some padding, or check the bounding box or
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    left-side-bearing of the first character to be displayed and set
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    the current point based on that.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://  Displaying a character:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    Compute the bounding box of the character. It will contain signed values
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    relative to <current_point, baseline>. I.e. if it returns x0,y0,x1,y1,
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    then the character should be displayed in the rectangle from
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    <current_point+SF*x0, baseline+SF*y0> to <current_point+SF*x1,baseline+SF*y1).
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://  Advancing for the next character:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    Call GlyphHMetrics, and compute 'current_point += SF * advance'.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// ADVANCED USAGE
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Quality:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    - Use the functions with Subpixel at the end to allow your characters
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      to have subpixel positioning. Since the font is anti-aliased, not
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      hinted, this is very import for quality. (This is not possible with
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      baked fonts.)
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    - Kerning is now supported, and if you're supporting subpixel rendering
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      then kerning is worth using to give your text a polished look.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Performance:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    - Convert Unicode codepoints to glyph indexes and operate on the glyphs;
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      if you don't do this, stb_truetype is forced to do the conversion on
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      every call.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://    - There are a lot of memory allocations. We should modify it to take
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      a temp buffer and allocate from the temp buffer (without freeing),
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://      should help performance a lot.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// NOTES
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   The system uses the raw data found in the .ttf file without changing it
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   and without building auxiliary data structures. This is a bit inefficient
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   on little-endian systems (the data is big-endian), but assuming you're
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   caching the bitmaps or glyph shapes this shouldn't be a big deal.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   It appears to be very hard to programmatically determine what font a
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   given file is in a general way. I provide an API for this, but I don't
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   recommend it.
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h:// PERFORMANCE MEASUREMENTS FOR 1.06:
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://                      32-bit     64-bit
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Previous release:  8.83 s     7.68 s
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Pool allocations:  7.72 s     6.34 s
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   Inline sort     :  6.54 s     5.65 s
include/stb-master/stb_truetype.h://   New rasterizer  :  5.63 s     5.00 s
