include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:/* stb_image_resize2 - v2.17 - public domain image resizing
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   by Jeff Roberts (v2) and Jorge L Rodriguez
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   http://github.com/nothings/stb
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   Can be threaded with the extended API. SSE2, AVX, Neon and WASM SIMD support. Only
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   scaling and translation is supported, no rotations or shears.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   COMPILING & LINKING
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      In one C/C++ file that #includes this file, do this:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         #define STB_IMAGE_RESIZE_IMPLEMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      before the #include. That will create the implementation in that file.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   EASY API CALLS:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     Easy API downsamples w/Mitchell filter, upsamples w/cubic interpolation, clamps to edge.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     stbir_resize_uint8_srgb( input_pixels,  input_w,  input_h,  input_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                              output_pixels, output_w, output_h, output_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                              pixel_layout_enum )
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     stbir_resize_uint8_linear( input_pixels,  input_w,  input_h,  input_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                                output_pixels, output_w, output_h, output_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                                pixel_layout_enum )
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     stbir_resize_float_linear( input_pixels,  input_w,  input_h,  input_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                                output_pixels, output_w, output_h, output_stride_in_bytes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:                                pixel_layout_enum )
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     If you pass NULL or zero for the output_pixels, we will allocate the output buffer
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     for you and return it from the function (free with free() or STBIR_FREE).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     As a special case, XX_stride_in_bytes of 0 means packed continuously in memory.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   API LEVELS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      There are three levels of API - easy-to-use, medium-complexity and extended-complexity.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      See the "header file" section of the source for API documentation.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:   ADDITIONAL DOCUMENTATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      MEMORY ALLOCATION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         By default, we use malloc and free for memory allocation.  To override the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         memory allocation, before the implementation #include, add a:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            #define STBIR_MALLOC(size,user_data) ...
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            #define STBIR_FREE(ptr,user_data)   ...
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Each resize makes exactly one call to malloc/free (unless you use the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         extended API where you can do one allocation for many resizes). Under
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         address sanitizer, we do separate allocations to find overread/writes.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      PERFORMANCE
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         This library was written with an emphasis on performance. When testing
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         stb_image_resize with RGBA, the fastest mode is STBIR_4CHANNEL with
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_TYPE_UINT8 pixels and CLAMPed edges (which is what many other resize
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         libs do by default). Also, make sure SIMD is turned on of course (default
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         for 64-bit targets). Avoid WRAP edge mode if you want the fastest speed.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         This library also comes with profiling built-in. If you define STBIR_PROFILE,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         you can use the advanced API and get low-level profiling information by
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         calling stbir_resize_extended_profile_info() or stbir_resize_split_profile_info()
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         after a resize.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      SIMD
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Most of the routines have optimized SSE2, AVX, NEON and WASM versions.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         On Microsoft compilers, we automatically turn on SIMD for 64-bit x64 and
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         ARM; for 32-bit x86 and ARM, you select SIMD mode by defining STBIR_SSE2 or
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_NEON. For AVX and AVX2, we auto-select it by detecting the /arch:AVX
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         or /arch:AVX2 switches. You can also always manually turn SSE2, AVX or AVX2
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         support on by defining STBIR_SSE2, STBIR_AVX or STBIR_AVX2.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         On Linux, SSE2 and Neon is on by default for 64-bit x64 or ARM64. For 32-bit,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         we select x86 SIMD mode by whether you have -msse2, -mavx or -mavx2 enabled
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         on the command line. For 32-bit ARM, you must pass -mfpu=neon-vfpv4 for both
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         clang and GCC, but GCC also requires an additional -mfp16-format=ieee to
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         automatically enable NEON.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         On x86 platforms, you can also define STBIR_FP16C to turn on FP16C instructions
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         for converting back and forth to half-floats. This is autoselected when we
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         are using AVX2. Clang and GCC also require the -mf16c switch. ARM always uses
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the built-in half float hardware NEON instructions.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         You can also tell us to use multiply-add instructions with STBIR_USE_FMA.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Because x86 doesn't always have fma, we turn it off by default to maintain
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         determinism across all platforms. If you don't care about non-FMA determinism
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         and are willing to restrict yourself to more recent x86 CPUs (around the AVX
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         timeframe), then fma will give you around a 15% speedup.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         You can force off SIMD in all cases by defining STBIR_NO_SIMD. You can turn
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         off AVX or AVX2 specifically with STBIR_NO_AVX or STBIR_NO_AVX2. AVX is 10%
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         to 40% faster, and AVX2 is generally another 12%.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      ALPHA CHANNEL
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Most of the resizing functions provide the ability to control how the alpha
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         channel of an image is processed.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         When alpha represents transparency, it is important that when combining
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         colors with filtering, the pixels should not be treated equally; they
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         should use a weighted average based on their alpha values. For example,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         if a pixel is 1% opaque bright green and another pixel is 99% opaque
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         black and you average them, the average will be 50% opaque, but the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         unweighted average and will be a middling green color, while the weighted
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         average will be nearly black. This means the unweighted version introduced
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         green energy that didn't exist in the source image.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         (If you want to know why this makes sense, you can work out the math for
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the following: consider what happens if you alpha composite a source image
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         over a fixed color and then average the output, vs. if you average the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         source image pixels and then composite that over the same fixed color.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Only the weighted average produces the same result as the ground truth
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         composite-then-average result.)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Therefore, it is in general best to "alpha weight" the pixels when applying
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         filters to them. This essentially means multiplying the colors by the alpha
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         values before combining them, and then dividing by the alpha value at the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         end.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         The computer graphics industry introduced a technique called "premultiplied
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         alpha" or "associated alpha" in which image colors are stored in image files
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         already multiplied by their alpha. This saves some math when compositing,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         and also avoids the need to divide by the alpha at the end (which is quite
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         inefficient). However, while premultiplied alpha is common in the movie CGI
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         industry, it is not commonplace in other industries like videogames, and most
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         consumer file formats are generally expected to contain not-premultiplied
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         colors. For example, Photoshop saves PNG files "unpremultiplied", and web
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         browsers like Chrome and Firefox expect PNG images to be unpremultiplied.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Note that there are three possibilities that might describe your image
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         and resize expectation:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:             1. images are not premultiplied, alpha weighting is desired
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:             2. images are not premultiplied, alpha weighting is not desired
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:             3. images are premultiplied
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Both case #2 and case #3 require the exact same math: no alpha weighting
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         should be applied or removed. Only case 1 requires extra math operations;
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the other two cases can be handled identically.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         stb_image_resize expects case #1 by default, applying alpha weighting to
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         images, expecting the input images to be unpremultiplied. This is what the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         COLOR+ALPHA buffer types tell the resizer to do.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         When you use the pixel layouts STBIR_RGBA, STBIR_BGRA, STBIR_ARGB,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_ABGR, STBIR_RX, or STBIR_XR you are telling us that the pixels are
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         non-premultiplied. In these cases, the resizer will alpha weight the colors
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         (effectively creating the premultiplied image), do the filtering, and then
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         convert back to non-premult on exit.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         When you use the pixel layouts STBIR_RGBA_PM, STBIR_RGBA_PM, STBIR_RGBA_PM,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_RGBA_PM, STBIR_RX_PM or STBIR_XR_PM, you are telling that the pixels
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         ARE premultiplied. In this case, the resizer doesn't have to do the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         premultipling - it can filter directly on the input. This about twice as
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         fast as the non-premultiplied case, so it's the right option if your data is
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         already setup correctly.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         When you use the pixel layout STBIR_4CHANNEL or STBIR_2CHANNEL, you are
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         telling us that there is no channel that represents transparency; it may be
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         RGB and some unrelated fourth channel that has been stored in the alpha
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         channel, but it is actually not alpha. No special processing will be
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         performed.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         The difference between the generic 4 or 2 channel layouts, and the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         specialized _PM versions is with the _PM versions you are telling us that
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the data *is* alpha, just don't premultiply it. That's important when
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         using SRGB pixel formats, we need to know where the alpha is, because
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         it is converted linearly (rather than with the SRGB converters).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Because alpha weighting produces the same effect as premultiplying, you
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         even have the option with non-premultiplied inputs to let the resizer
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         produce a premultiplied output. Because the intially computed alpha-weighted
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         output image is effectively premultiplied, this is actually more performant
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         than the normal path which un-premultiplies the output image as a final step.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Finally, when converting both in and out of non-premulitplied space (for
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         example, when using STBIR_RGBA), we go to somewhat heroic measures to
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         ensure that areas with zero alpha value pixels get something reasonable
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         in the RGB values. If you don't care about the RGB values of zero alpha
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         pixels, you can call the stbir_set_non_pm_alpha_speed_over_quality()
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         function - this runs a premultiplied resize about 25% faster. That said,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         when you really care about speed, using premultiplied pixels for both in
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         and out (STBIR_RGBA_PM, etc) much faster than both of these premultiplied
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         options.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      PIXEL LAYOUT CONVERSION
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         The resizer can convert from some pixel layouts to others. When using the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         stbir_set_pixel_layouts(), you can, for example, specify STBIR_RGBA
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         on input, and STBIR_ARGB on output, and it will re-organize the channels
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         during the resize. Currently, you can only convert between two pixel
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         layouts with the same number of channels.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      DETERMINISM
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         We commit to being deterministic (from x64 to ARM to scalar to SIMD, etc).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         This requires compiling with fast-math off (using at least /fp:precise).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Also, you must turn off fp-contracting (which turns mult+adds into fmas)!
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         We attempt to do this with pragmas, but with Clang, you usually want to add
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         -ffp-contract=off to the command line as well.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         For 32-bit x86, you must use SSE and SSE2 codegen for determinism. That is,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         if the scalar x87 unit gets used at all, we immediately lose determinism.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         On Microsoft Visual Studio 2008 and earlier, from what we can tell there is
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         no way to be deterministic in 32-bit x86 (some x87 always leaks in, even
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         with fp:strict). On 32-bit x86 GCC, determinism requires both -msse2 and
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         -fpmath=sse.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Note that we will not be deterministic with float data containing NaNs -
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the NaNs will propagate differently on different SIMD and platforms.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         If you turn on STBIR_USE_FMA, then we will be deterministic with other
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         fma targets, but we will differ from non-fma targets (this is unavoidable,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         because a fma isn't simply an add with a mult - it also introduces a
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         rounding difference compared to non-fma instruction sequences.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      FLOAT PIXEL FORMAT RANGE
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Any range of values can be used for the non-alpha float data that you pass
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         in (0 to 1, -1 to 1, whatever). However, if you are inputting float values
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         but *outputting* bytes or shorts, you must use a range of 0 to 1 so that we
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         scale back properly. The alpha channel must also be 0 to 1 for any format
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         that does premultiplication prior to resizing.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Note also that with float output, using filters with negative lobes, the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         output filtered values might go slightly out of range. You can define
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_FLOAT_LOW_CLAMP and/or STBIR_FLOAT_HIGH_CLAMP to specify the range
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         to clamp to on output, if that's important.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      MAX/MIN SCALE FACTORS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         The input pixel resolutions are in integers, and we do the internal pointer
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         resolution in size_t sized integers. However, the scale ratio from input
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         resolution to output resolution is calculated in float form. This means
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         the effective possible scale ratio is limited to 24 bits (or 16 million
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         to 1). As you get close to the size of the float resolution (again, 16
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         million pixels wide or high), you might start seeing float inaccuracy
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         issues in general in the pipeline. If you have to do extreme resizes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         you can usually do this is multiple stages (using float intermediate
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         buffers).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      FLIPPED IMAGES
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Stride is just the delta from one scanline to the next. This means you can
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         use a negative stride to handle inverted images (point to the final
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         scanline and use a negative stride). You can invert the input or output,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         using negative strides.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      DEFAULT FILTERS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         For functions which don't provide explicit control over what filters to
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         use, you can change the compile-time defaults with:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            #define STBIR_DEFAULT_FILTER_UPSAMPLE     STBIR_FILTER_something
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            #define STBIR_DEFAULT_FILTER_DOWNSAMPLE   STBIR_FILTER_something
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         See stbir_filter in the header-file section for the list of filters.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      NEW FILTERS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         A number of 1D filter kernels are supplied. For a list of supported
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         filters, see the stbir_filter enum. You can install your own filters by
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         using the stbir_set_filter_callbacks function.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      PROGRESS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         For interactive use with slow resize operations, you can use the 
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         scanline callbacks in the extended API. It would have to be a *very* large
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         image resample to need progress though - we're very fast.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      CEIL and FLOOR
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         In scalar mode, the only functions we use from math.h are ceilf and floorf,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         but if you have your own versions, you can define the STBIR_CEILF(v) and
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         STBIR_FLOORF(v) macros and we'll use them instead. In SIMD, we just use
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         our own versions.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      ASSERT
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         Define STBIR_ASSERT(boolval) to override assert() and not use assert.h
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:     PORTING FROM VERSION 1
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        The API has changed. You can continue to use the old version of stb_image_resize.h,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        which is available in the "deprecated/" directory.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        If you're using the old simple-to-use API, porting is straightforward.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        (For more advanced APIs, read the documentation.)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:          stbir_resize_uint8():
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - call `stbir_resize_uint8_linear`, cast channel count to `stbir_pixel_layout`
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:          stbir_resize_float():
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - call `stbir_resize_float_linear`, cast channel count to `stbir_pixel_layout`
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:          stbir_resize_uint8_srgb():
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - function name is unchanged
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - cast channel count to `stbir_pixel_layout`
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - above is sufficient unless your image has alpha and it's not RGBA/BGRA
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:              - in that case, follow the below instructions for stbir_resize_uint8_srgb_edgemode
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:          stbir_resize_uint8_srgb_edgemode()
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - switch to the "medium complexity" API
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - stbir_resize(), very similar API but a few more parameters:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:              - pixel_layout: cast channel count to `stbir_pixel_layout`
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:              - data_type:    STBIR_TYPE_UINT8_SRGB
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:              - edge:         unchanged (STBIR_EDGE_WRAP, etc.)
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:              - filter:       STBIR_FILTER_DEFAULT
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:            - which channel is alpha is specified in stbir_pixel_layout, see enum for details
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:      FUTURE TODOS
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        *  For polyphase integral filters, we just memcpy the coeffs to dupe
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           them, but we should indirect and use the same coeff memory.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:        *  Add pixel layout conversions for sensible different channel counts
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           (maybe, 1->3/4, 3->4, 4->1, 3->1).
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * For SIMD encode and decode scanline routines, do any pre-aligning
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           for bad input/output buffer alignments and pitch?
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * For very wide scanlines, we should we do vertical strips to stay within
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           L2 cache. Maybe do chunks of 1K pixels at a time. There would be
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           some pixel reconversion, but probably dwarfed by things falling out
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           of cache. Probably also something possible with alternating between
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           scattering and gathering at high resize scales?
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * Should we have a multiple MIPs at the same time function (could keep
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           more memory in cache during multiple resizes)?
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * Rewrite the coefficient generator to do many at once.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * AVX-512 vertical kernels - worried about downclocking here.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * Convert the reincludes to macros when we know they aren't changing.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * Experiment with pivoting the horizontal and always using the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           vertical filters (which are faster, but perhaps not enough to overcome
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           the pivot cost and the extra memory touches). Need to buffer the whole
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           image so have to balance memory use.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:         * Most of our code is internally function pointers, should we compile
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:           all the SIMD stuff always and dynamically dispatch?
