include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h:// Figure out whether to scale along the horizontal or vertical first.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   This only *super* important when you are scaling by a massively
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   different amount in the vertical vs the horizontal (for example, if
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   you are scaling by 2x in the width, and 0.5x in the height, then you
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   want to do the vertical scale first, because it's around 3x faster
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   in that order.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   In more normal circumstances, this makes a 20-40% differences, so
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     it's good to get right, but not critical. The normal way that you
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     decide which direction goes first is just figuring out which
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     direction does more multiplies. But with modern CPUs with their
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     fancy caches and SIMD and high IPC abilities, so there's just a lot
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     more that goes into it.
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://   My handwavy sort of solution is to have an app that does a whole
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     bunch of timing for both vertical and horizontal first modes,
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     and then another app that can read lots of these timing files
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     and try to search for the best weights to use. Dotimings.c
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     is the app that does a bunch of timings, and vf_train.c is the
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     app that solves for the best weights (and shows how well it
include/stb-master/stb_image_resize2.h://     does currently).
