include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   Fabian Giesen: secondary key inputs in 1.5
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   Martins Mozeiko: STB_TEXTEDIT_memmove in 1.6
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   Louis Schnellbach: page up/down in 1.14
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   Bugfixes:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      Scott Graham
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      Daniel Keller
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      Omar Cornut
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      Dan Thompson
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// USAGE
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// This file behaves differently depending on what symbols you define
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// before including it.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Header-file mode:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   If you do not define STB_TEXTEDIT_IMPLEMENTATION before including this,
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   it will operate in "header file" mode. In this mode, it declares a
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   single public symbol, STB_TexteditState, which encapsulates the current
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   state of a text widget (except for the string, which you will store
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   separately).
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   To compile in this mode, you must define STB_TEXTEDIT_CHARTYPE to a
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   primitive type that defines a single character (e.g. char, wchar_t, etc).
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   To save space or increase undo-ability, you can optionally define the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   following things that are used by the undo system:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      STB_TEXTEDIT_POSITIONTYPE         small int type encoding a valid cursor position
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOSTATECOUNT       the number of undo states to allow
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOCHARCOUNT        the number of characters to store in the undo buffer
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   If you don't define these, they are set to permissive types and
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   moderate sizes. The undo system does no memory allocations, so
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   it grows STB_TexteditState by the worst-case storage which is (in bytes):
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://        [4 + 3 * sizeof(STB_TEXTEDIT_POSITIONTYPE)] * STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOSTATECOUNT
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      +          sizeof(STB_TEXTEDIT_CHARTYPE)      * STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOCHARCOUNT
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Implementation mode:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   If you define STB_TEXTEDIT_IMPLEMENTATION before including this, it
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   will compile the implementation of the text edit widget, depending
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   on a large number of symbols which must be defined before the include.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   The implementation is defined only as static functions. You will then
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   need to provide your own APIs in the same file which will access the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   static functions.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   The basic concept is that you provide a "string" object which
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   behaves like an array of characters. stb_textedit uses indices to
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   refer to positions in the string, implicitly representing positions
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   in the displayed textedit. This is true for both plain text and
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   rich text; even with rich text stb_truetype interacts with your
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   code as if there was an array of all the displayed characters.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Symbols that must be the same in header-file and implementation mode:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://     STB_TEXTEDIT_CHARTYPE             the character type
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://     STB_TEXTEDIT_POSITIONTYPE         small type that is a valid cursor position
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://     STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOSTATECOUNT       the number of undo states to allow
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://     STB_TEXTEDIT_UNDOCHARCOUNT        the number of characters to store in the undo buffer
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Symbols you must define for implementation mode:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING               the type of object representing a string being edited,
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                      typically this is a wrapper object with other data you need
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_STRINGLEN(obj)       the length of the string (ideally O(1))
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_LAYOUTROW(&r,obj,n)  returns the results of laying out a line of characters
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        starting from character #n (see discussion below)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_GETWIDTH(obj,n,i)    returns the pixel delta from the xpos of the i'th character
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        to the xpos of the i+1'th char for a line of characters
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        starting at character #n (i.e. accounts for kerning
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        with previous char)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_KEYTOTEXT(k)         maps a keyboard input to an insertable character
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        (return type is int, -1 means not valid to insert)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_GETCHAR(obj,i)       returns the i'th character of obj, 0-based
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_NEWLINE              the character returned by _GETCHAR() we recognize
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                        as manually wordwrapping for end-of-line positioning
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_DELETECHARS(obj,i,n)      delete n characters starting at i
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_INSERTCHARS(obj,i,c*,n)   insert n characters at i (pointed to by STB_TEXTEDIT_CHARTYPE*)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_SHIFT       a power of two that is or'd in to a keyboard input to represent the shift key
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LEFT        keyboard input to move cursor left
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_RIGHT       keyboard input to move cursor right
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_UP          keyboard input to move cursor up
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_DOWN        keyboard input to move cursor down
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_PGUP        keyboard input to move cursor up a page
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_PGDOWN      keyboard input to move cursor down a page
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LINESTART   keyboard input to move cursor to start of line  // e.g. HOME
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LINEEND     keyboard input to move cursor to end of line    // e.g. END
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_TEXTSTART   keyboard input to move cursor to start of text  // e.g. ctrl-HOME
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_TEXTEND     keyboard input to move cursor to end of text    // e.g. ctrl-END
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_DELETE      keyboard input to delete selection or character under cursor
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_BACKSPACE   keyboard input to delete selection or character left of cursor
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_UNDO        keyboard input to perform undo
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_REDO        keyboard input to perform redo
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Optional:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_INSERT              keyboard input to toggle insert mode
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_IS_SPACE(ch)          true if character is whitespace (e.g. 'isspace'),
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://                                          required for default WORDLEFT/WORDRIGHT handlers
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_MOVEWORDLEFT(obj,i)   custom handler for WORDLEFT, returns index to move cursor to
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_MOVEWORDRIGHT(obj,i)  custom handler for WORDRIGHT, returns index to move cursor to
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_WORDLEFT            keyboard input to move cursor left one word // e.g. ctrl-LEFT
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_WORDRIGHT           keyboard input to move cursor right one word // e.g. ctrl-RIGHT
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LINESTART2          secondary keyboard input to move cursor to start of line
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LINEEND2            secondary keyboard input to move cursor to end of line
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_TEXTSTART2          secondary keyboard input to move cursor to start of text
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    STB_TEXTEDIT_K_TEXTEND2            secondary keyboard input to move cursor to end of text
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Keyboard input must be encoded as a single integer value; e.g. a character code
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// and some bitflags that represent shift states. to simplify the interface, SHIFT must
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// be a bitflag, so we can test the shifted state of cursor movements to allow selection,
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// i.e. (STB_TEXTEDIT_K_RIGHT|STB_TEXTEDIT_K_SHIFT) should be shifted right-arrow.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// You can encode other things, such as CONTROL or ALT, in additional bits, and
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// then test for their presence in e.g. STB_TEXTEDIT_K_WORDLEFT. For example,
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// my Windows implementations add an additional CONTROL bit, and an additional KEYDOWN
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// bit. Then all of the STB_TEXTEDIT_K_ values bitwise-or in the KEYDOWN bit,
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// and I pass both WM_KEYDOWN and WM_CHAR events to the "key" function in the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// API below. The control keys will only match WM_KEYDOWN events because of the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// keydown bit I add, and STB_TEXTEDIT_KEYTOTEXT only tests for the KEYDOWN
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// bit so it only decodes WM_CHAR events.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// STB_TEXTEDIT_LAYOUTROW returns information about the shape of one displayed
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// row of characters assuming they start on the i'th character--the width and
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// the height and the number of characters consumed. This allows this library
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// to traverse the entire layout incrementally. You need to compute word-wrapping
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// here.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Each textfield keeps its own insert mode state, which is not how normal
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// applications work. To keep an app-wide insert mode, update/copy the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// "insert_mode" field of STB_TexteditState before/after calling API functions.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// API
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    void stb_textedit_initialize_state(STB_TexteditState *state, int is_single_line)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    void stb_textedit_click(STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING *str, STB_TexteditState *state, float x, float y)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    void stb_textedit_drag(STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING *str, STB_TexteditState *state, float x, float y)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    int  stb_textedit_cut(STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING *str, STB_TexteditState *state)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    int  stb_textedit_paste(STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING *str, STB_TexteditState *state, STB_TEXTEDIT_CHARTYPE *text, int len)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    void stb_textedit_key(STB_TEXTEDIT_STRING *str, STB_TexteditState *state, STB_TEXEDIT_KEYTYPE key)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    Each of these functions potentially updates the string and updates the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://    state.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      initialize_state:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          set the textedit state to a known good default state when initially
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          constructing the textedit.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      click:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          call this with the mouse x,y on a mouse down; it will update the cursor
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          and reset the selection start/end to the cursor point. the x,y must
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          be relative to the text widget, with (0,0) being the top left.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      drag:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          call this with the mouse x,y on a mouse drag/up; it will update the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          cursor and the selection end point
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      cut:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          call this to delete the current selection; returns true if there was
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          one. you should FIRST copy the current selection to the system paste buffer.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          (To copy, just copy the current selection out of the string yourself.)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      paste:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          call this to paste text at the current cursor point or over the current
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          selection if there is one.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://      key:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          call this for keyboard inputs sent to the textfield. you can use it
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          for "key down" events or for "translated" key events. if you need to
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          do both (as in Win32), or distinguish Unicode characters from control
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          inputs, set a high bit to distinguish the two; then you can define the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          various definitions like STB_TEXTEDIT_K_LEFT have the is-key-event bit
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          set, and make STB_TEXTEDIT_KEYTOCHAR check that the is-key-event bit is
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          clear. STB_TEXTEDIT_KEYTYPE defaults to int, but you can #define it to
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://          anything other type you wante before including.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   When rendering, you can read the cursor position and selection state from
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://   the STB_TexteditState.
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// Notes:
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// This is designed to be usable in IMGUI, so it allows for the possibility of
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// running in an IMGUI that has NOT cached the multi-line layout. For this
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// reason, it provides an interface that is compatible with computing the
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// layout incrementally--we try to make sure we make as few passes through
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// as possible. (For example, to locate the mouse pointer in the text, we
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// could define functions that return the X and Y positions of characters
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// and binary search Y and then X, but if we're doing dynamic layout this
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// will run the layout algorithm many times, so instead we manually search
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// forward in one pass. Similar logic applies to e.g. up-arrow and
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// down-arrow movement.)
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h://
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// If it's run in a widget that *has* cached the layout, then this is less
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// efficient, but it's not horrible on modern computers. But you wouldn't
include/stb-master/stb_textedit.h:// want to edit million-line files with it.
