include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          string hashmap which will automatically allocate and free
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          each string key using realloc/free
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:      sh_new_arena
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        void sh_new_arena(T*);
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          Overwrites the existing pointer with a newly allocated
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          string hashmap which will automatically allocate each string
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          key to a string arena. Every string key ever used by this
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          hash table remains in the arena until the arena is freed.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          Additionally, any key which is deleted and reinserted will
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:          be allocated multiple times in the string arena.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:NOTES
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * These data structures are realloc'd when they grow, and the macro
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    "functions" write to the provided pointer. This means: (a) the pointer
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    must be an lvalue, and (b) the pointer to the data structure is not
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    stable, and you must maintain it the same as you would a realloc'd
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    pointer. For example, if you pass a pointer to a dynamic array to a
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    function which updates it, the function must return back the new
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    pointer to the caller. This is the price of trying to do this in C.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * The following are the only functions that are thread-safe on a single data
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    structure, i.e. can be run in multiple threads simultaneously on the same
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    data structure
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        hmlen        shlen
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        hmlenu       shlenu
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        hmget_ts     shget_ts
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        hmgeti_ts    shgeti_ts
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:        hmgets_ts    shgets_ts
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * You iterate over the contents of a dynamic array and a hashmap in exactly
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    the same way, using arrlen/hmlen/shlen:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:      for (i=0; i < arrlen(foo); ++i)
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:         ... foo[i] ...
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * All operations except arrins/arrdel are O(1) amortized, but individual
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    operations can be slow, so these data structures may not be suitable
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    for real time use. Dynamic arrays double in capacity as needed, so
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    elements are copied an average of once. Hash tables double/halve
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    their size as needed, with appropriate hysteresis to maintain O(1)
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    performance.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:NOTES - DYNAMIC ARRAY
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * If you know how long a dynamic array is going to be in advance, you can avoid
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    extra memory allocations by using arrsetlen to allocate it to that length in
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    advance and use foo[n] while filling it out, or arrsetcap to allocate the memory
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    for that length and use arrput/arrpush as normal.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * Unlike some other versions of the dynamic array, this version should
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    be safe to use with strict-aliasing optimizations.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:NOTES - HASH MAP
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * For compilers other than GCC and clang (e.g. Visual Studio), for hmput/hmget/hmdel
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    and variants, the key must be an lvalue (so the macro can take the address of it).
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Extensions are used that eliminate this requirement if you're using C99 and later
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    in GCC or clang, or if you're using C++ in GCC. But note that this can make your
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    code less portable.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * To test for presence of a key in a hashmap, just do 'hmgeti(foo,key) >= 0'.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * The iteration order of your data in the hashmap is determined solely by the
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    order of insertions and deletions. In particular, if you never delete, new
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    keys are always added at the end of the array. This will be consistent
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    across all platforms and versions of the library. However, you should not
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    attempt to serialize the internal hash table, as the hash is not consistent
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    between different platforms, and may change with future versions of the library.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * Use sh_new_arena() for string hashmaps that you never delete from. Initialize
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    with NULL if you're managing the memory for your strings, or your strings are
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    never freed (at least until the hashmap is freed). Otherwise, use sh_new_strdup().
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    @TODO: make an arena variant that garbage collects the strings with a trivial
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    copy collector into a new arena whenever the table shrinks / rebuilds. Since
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    current arena recommendation is to only use arena if it never deletes, then
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    this can just replace current arena implementation.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * If adversarial input is a serious concern and you're on a 64-bit platform,
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    enable STBDS_SIPHASH_2_4 (see the 'Compile-time options' section), and pass
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    a strong random number to stbds_rand_seed.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  * The default value for the hash table is stored in foo[-1], so if you
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    use code like 'hmget(T,k)->value = 5' you can accidentally overwrite
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    the value stored by hmdefault if 'k' is not present.
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:CREDITS
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  Sean Barrett -- library, idea for dynamic array API/implementation
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  Per Vognsen  -- idea for hash table API/implementation
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  Rafael Sachetto -- arrpop()
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  github:HeroicKatora -- arraddn() reworking
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:  Bugfixes:
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Andy Durdin
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Shane Liesegang
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Vinh Truong
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Andreas Molzer
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    github:hashitaku
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    github:srdjanstipic
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Macoy Madson
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Andreas Vennstrom
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:    Tobias Mansfield-Williams
include/stb-master/stb_ds.h:*/
